%!PS-Adobe-3.0
%%Title: hatchkit.ps — reusable hatching and cross-hatching primitives
%%Creator: pscat — issue #49
%%EndComments

% A sixth sibling to graph.ps/dataviz.ps/etching.ps/pagekit.ps/paintkit.ps
% (no dependency on any of them, like graph/dataviz/etching): a single
% operator, `hatch`, that fills whatever region is currently clipped
% with a family of parallel line strokes. Pen-and-ink shading,
% engraving, architectural sketching, comics, and photo-to-sketch
% rendering all lean on the same underlying vocabulary item; this is
% that item, not a rendering pipeline of its own — the caller supplies
% the region (via an ordinary `clip`) and, optionally, a tone-driving
% callback. `lib/etching.ps`'s `et-hatch` already draws exactly this
% kind of parallel-line pass over an image's tone map; `hatch`
% generalizes that trick's *geometry* into something reusable, image
% analysis and tone extraction stay out of scope (etching.ps's job).
%
%   << /Angle 30 /Spacing 4 >> hatch
%
% `hatch` does not clip on its own. Set the region you want filled as
% the current clip *before* calling it — `<path> clip` — the same way
% `et-draw` clips to the page rect before its own hatch passes. Lines
% are drawn well past the region's actual boundary and rely entirely
% on the graphics state's real clip to cut them to shape; `hatch`
% never reimplements point-in-polygon or edge-crossing tests, so it
% clips to concave and self-intersecting paths exactly as well as
% convex ones — whatever the interpreter's own `clip` already handles.
% This also sidesteps `clippath`'s known limitation (issue #120: it
% reports only the newest of several nested clips, not their true
% intersection) — `hatch` never calls `clippath` at all.
%
% Options, all optional except that a region must be establishable
% one of two ways:
%   /BBox [x0 y0 x1 y1]  the area to sweep lines across. If omitted,
%           `hatch` reads it from `pathbbox` — the *current path's*
%           bounding box — so the usual call shape is
%           `<path> clip << ... >> hatch` (`clip` does not consume the
%           current path, so it is still there for `pathbbox` to read;
%           `hatch` itself brackets its own drawing in `gsave`/
%           `grestore`, so the path survives a call too — a second,
%           layered `hatch` call over the same clip, the classic
%           cross-hatch composition, can keep relying on the default).
%           A caller who clips from a path they no longer hold (built
%           earlier, or via `clippath` itself) must pass /BBox
%           explicitly. Either way the box only decides how many
%           candidate lines get swept and where each one starts and
%           ends before clipping — the real clip alone decides what
%           actually inks the page, so a loose /BBox costs a few wasted
%           candidates, never wrong output; a tight one is more
%           efficient, never more correct.
%   /Angle deg  a single hatch angle, degrees, 0 = horizontal (default
%           45). Ignored when /Angles is given.
%   /Angles [deg ...]  multiple angles, each swept as a full
%           independent pass in array order — the mechanism behind
%           cross-hatching (two angles 90 degrees apart) and
%           multi-angle engraving fills (three or more). The random
%           stream is consumed one full pass at a time, in this order,
%           so reproducibility holds per angle as well as overall.
%   /Spacing num  device units between adjacent lines within one pass;
%           must be > 0 (default 6).
%   /Width num-or-[lo hi]  stroke width. A single number draws every
%           line at that width (unless /Density overrides it — see
%           below). A [lo hi] range draws one line's width uniformly
%           from that range — one random draw per drawn line, so a
%           /Width number vs. range changes how many rolls a line
%           consumes, which matters for reproducing an exact seeded
%           arrangement. Default 1.
%   /Wobble num >= 0  maximum random perpendicular offset applied to
%           each line's position, drawn uniformly from
%           [-Wobble, Wobble] (default 0, off — an /Wobble of 0
%           consumes no roll at all, same reproducibility note as
%           /Width). This is what keeps a hatch from reading as a
%           mechanically perfect grid; it does not bend a line's
%           course mid-stroke — for a genuinely shaky hand-drawn
%           stroke, layer `lib/paintkit.ps`'s ribbon strokes instead.
%   /Dropout num in [0,1]  probability a given candidate line is
%           skipped entirely (default 0, off — like /Wobble and
%           /Trim, a /Dropout of 0 consumes no roll at all). Rolled
%           first, before any other roll for that line, so a kept
%           line's later rolls (/Wobble, /Trim, /Width) happen in the
%           same relative order whether or not /Dropout is in play.
%   /Trim [lo hi]  fraction, in [0,1], of a drawn line's own
%           *in-region* length (the part `hatch` actually clips into
%           the BBox — not the full sweep) to shorten it by, split at a
%           second random point between the two ends. Only rolled when
%           the range's high end is > 0, two rolls (the fraction, then
%           the front/back split) — the "length variation" that keeps
%           hatch strokes from all reaching the region's edge in
%           lockstep. Default [0 0], off.
%   /Density {x y -> d}  optional per-sample tone callback, called with
%           a point along each line (in the same user-space coordinates
%           as /BBox). MUST consume both operands and leave exactly one
%           number (a proc that doesn't leaks two per sample — see the
%           "Scratch prefix" note below) and must not touch any hk-
%           prefixed name. The result is
%           clamped into [0,1] regardless of what the proc actually
%           returns, so a callback that returns something wild changes
%           the *tone*, never the amount of geometry drawn. When given,
%           /Width's range (or its single number used for both ends)
%           is interpolated by the clamped density into 6 fixed stroke-
%           width buckets, quantized into runs — one stroke per
%           constant-bucket run along the line, not one per sample,
%           the same technique `lib/etching.ps`'s `et-hatch` uses and
%           for the same reason (stroke count, not sample count,
%           dominates render time). A sample whose clamped density is
%           <= /DensityThreshold draws no ink for that run at all —
%           this is what lets a density field carve a shape's lit side
%           out of the hatching entirely, not just thin it.
%   /DensityThreshold num in [0,1]  see /Density (default 0).
%   /Seed n  seeds the sweep with `n srand`, restoring the caller's own
%           random stream afterward via `rrand` — the same convention
%           `lib/artkit.ps`'s `scatter` documents, including the same
%           caveat: the restore is skipped if the call errors out.
%           Omit it to draw from the ambient stream, still
%           deterministic under the caller's own `N srand`.
%   /MaxLines n  hard cap on the total candidate line count summed
%           across every angle in /Angles (default 20000). This count
%           is fully determined by /BBox, /Spacing, and /Angles alone
%           — no candidate is ever drawn or sampled before it is
%           checked against this cap, so a pathologically small
%           /Spacing over a large /BBox is rejected
%           (`hatch-line-count-exceeds-safety-limit`) before anything
%           happens, the same up-front-reject shape `scatter`'s own
%           /Budget uses.
%   /MaxSamples n  hard cap on the total /Density callback invocations
%           summed across every angle (default 2000000), checked only
%           when /Density is given (a call with no /Density never
%           samples anything, so this bound is moot for it) and, like
%           /MaxLines, computed purely from /BBox/Spacing/Angles before
%           any drawing — an upper bound on each line's own in-box
%           span, not the full sweep, so it tracks actual sampling work
%           rather than the (up to 2x larger) diagonal a cruder estimate
%           would use. This is the bound that actually keeps a
%           /Density callback from creating unbounded output: a
%           misbehaving callback can only change *what* gets sampled,
%           never *how many* samples are taken
%           (`hatch-sample-count-exceeds-safety-limit`).
%
% Reproducibility: for a given /BBox, /Angles order, and seed, the same
% options draw the same picture, in this interpreter. As with every
% other rand-driven procedure in this codebase, that pinning is local
% to this interpreter's own `rand` — Ghostscript uses a different
% generator, so a seeded hatch renders as a different, equally valid,
% equally reproducible arrangement there; only straight-edged geometry
% (this operator never flattens curves) is otherwise identical between
% the two.
%
% Scratch prefix: hk- throughout (internal helpers and `hatch`'s own
% working state alike) — a single self-contained file, unlike
% artkit.ps's multi-section sc-/sq-/si- split, since /Density (the one
% caller-supplied proc here) never itself calls back into a public
% `hatch`- prefixed name the way scatter's /Weight calls `scin`
% mid-loop, so there is no second scratch prefix to keep separate from
% the caller's own state. /Density is still called mid-loop, though,
% with the same two obligations `scatter`'s own /Mark and /Weight
% procs document: it MUST consume both of its operands (a proc that
% doesn't leaks two numbers per sample onto the operand stack —
% `--lint` reports exactly that), and it must not touch any hk-
% prefixed name, since `hatch` resumes reading its own hk- scratch
% immediately after the call returns.

% @internal
/hkfrnd { rand 8192 idiv 262143 and 262143 div } def % -> 0.0..1.0

% dict key default hkgetdef -> value
% Same wrap-the-default-in-an-array trick as artkit's scgetdef /
% paintkit's pkgetdef / pagekit's pggetdef: a name bound directly to a
% procedure auto-executes on bare reference, so the default travels
% inside a 1-element array instead.
% @internal
/hkgetdef {
  /hkgdd exch 1 array astore def
  /hkgdk exch def
  /hkgdt exch def
  hkgdt hkgdk known { hkgdt hkgdk get } { hkgdd 0 get } ifelse
} def

% v hkclamp01 -> v', clamped into [0,1]
% @internal
/hkclamp01 {
  dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if
  dup 1 gt { pop 1 } if
} def

% a b t hklerp -> value, a + (b-a)*t
% @internal
/hklerp {
  /hkt exch def
  /hkb exch def
  /hka exch def
  hka hkb hka sub hkt mul add
} def

% Running min/max over a handful of fed values — hkmmreset then
% hkmmfeed per value, hkmn/hkmx hold the result. Named-variable style
% (not a generic reduce) on purpose: this file has exactly one caller
% of it (the per-angle BBox-corner projection below) and four values
% every time.
% @internal
/hkmmreset {
  /hkmn 1.0e30 def
  /hkmx -1.0e30 def
} def

% v hkmmfeed -
% @internal
/hkmmfeed {
  /hkv exch def
  hkv hkmn lt { /hkmn hkv def } if
  hkv hkmx gt { /hkmx hkv def } if
} def

% p q hkclip1 -  (mutates hktlo/hkthi/hkhit)
% One boundary of a Liang-Barsky parametric line clip: p t <= q. p==0
% (the line runs parallel to this boundary) skips straight through —
% by construction every call site below only ever sweeps offsets whose
% infinite line already intersects the box (see hkclipseg), so the
% p==0-and-outside rejection branch a general-purpose Liang-Barsky
% needs never actually fires here; hkhit exists as a defensive
% backstop, not a load-bearing path.
% @internal
/hkclip1 {
  /hkq exch def
  /hkp exch def
  hkp 0 eq {
    hkq 0 lt { /hkhit false def } if
  } {
    hkq hkp div /hkr exch def
    hkp 0 lt
      { hkr hktlo gt { /hktlo hkr def } if }
      { hkr hkthi lt { /hkthi hkr def } if }
    ifelse
  } ifelse
} def

% px py dx dy tlo thi hkclipseg -> tlo' thi' hit
% Clips the parametric line (px,py) + t*(dx,dy), t in [tlo,thi], against
% the box (hbx0,hby0)-(hbx1,hby1) — the four boundary tests unrolled
% rather than looped, since there are always exactly four and this
% keeps every term legible. Every candidate offset this file feeds in
% (see the /BBox-corner projection in `hatch`) is chosen so the box's
% projection onto the sweep normal already contains it, which by
% convexity guarantees a genuine (possibly zero-length, at a corner
% graze) intersection — so hit is a defensive final check, not
% something normal calls are expected to fail.
% @internal
/hkclipseg {
  /hkthi exch def
  /hktlo exch def
  /hkdy exch def
  /hkdx exch def
  /hkpy exch def
  /hkpx exch def
  /hkhit true def
  hkdx neg hkpx hbx0 sub hkclip1
  hkdx hbx1 hkpx sub hkclip1
  hkdy neg hkpy hby0 sub hkclip1
  hkdy hby1 hkpy sub hkclip1
  hktlo hkthi gt { /hkhit false def } if
  hktlo hkthi hkhit
} def

% opts hatch -
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Fills the current clip region with a family of parallel hatch lines — single or multi-angle, with seeded width, wobble, dropout, and length variation, and an optional per-point density callback for tonal shading. Does not clip on its own; set the region as the current clip before calling.
% @example: newpath 20 20 moveto 180 20 lineto 180 180 lineto 20 180 lineto closepath clip << /Angle 30 /Spacing 4 /Seed 7 >> hatch
% @param: /BBox [x0 y0 x1 y1] area to sweep; defaults to `pathbbox` of the current path
% @param: /Angle single hatch angle in degrees (default 45), ignored when /Angles is given
% @param: /Angles [deg ...] one full pass per angle, in order — cross-hatching and multi-angle fills
% @param: /Spacing device units between lines within a pass; must be > 0 (default 6)
% @param: /Width num or [lo hi] stroke width, or its range for a per-line uniform draw (default 1)
% @param: /Wobble num >= 0 max random perpendicular offset per line (default 0)
% @param: /Dropout num in [0,1] probability a candidate line is skipped (default 0)
% @param: /Trim [lo hi] fraction of a line's in-region length to shorten by, split randomly between ends (default [0 0])
% @param: /Density {x y -> d} optional per-sample tone callback in [0,1] (clamped); drives width buckets and a no-ink cutoff
% @param: /DensityThreshold num in [0,1] samples at or below this draw no ink (default 0)
% @param: /Seed n seeds with `n srand`, restores the caller's stream afterward via `rrand`
% @param: /MaxLines n hard cap on total candidate lines across all angles, checked before drawing (default 20000)
% @param: /MaxSamples n hard cap on total /Density calls across all angles, checked before drawing (default 2000000)
/hatch {
  /hopts exch def

  hopts /BBox known
    { hopts /BBox get aload pop }
    { pathbbox }
  ifelse
  /hby1 exch def
  /hbx1 exch def
  /hby0 exch def
  /hbx0 exch def
  hbx1 hbx0 le hby1 hby0 le or { hatch-bbox-degenerate } if
  /hbw hbx1 hbx0 sub def
  /hbh hby1 hby0 sub def
  /hdiag hbw dup mul hbh dup mul add sqrt def
  /hcx hbx0 hbx1 add 2 div def
  /hcy hby0 hby1 add 2 div def

  hopts /Spacing 6 hkgetdef /hspacing exch def
  hspacing 0 le { hatch-spacing-must-be-positive } if
  /hstep hspacing 0.5 mul def
  hstep 0.25 lt { /hstep 0.25 def } if

  hopts /Angles known
    { hopts /Angles get }
    { [ hopts /Angle 45 hkgetdef ] }
  ifelse
  /hangles exch def

  hopts /Width 1 hkgetdef /hwidthopt exch def
  hwidthopt type /arraytype eq
    {
      /hwidthlo hwidthopt 0 get def
      /hwidthhi hwidthopt 1 get def
      /hwidthranged true def
    }
    {
      /hwidthlo hwidthopt def
      /hwidthhi hwidthopt def
      /hwidthranged false def
    }
  ifelse

  hopts /Wobble 0 hkgetdef /hwobble exch def
  hopts /Dropout 0 hkgetdef /hdropout exch def
  hopts /Trim [ 0 0 ] hkgetdef /htrimopt exch def
  /htrimlo htrimopt 0 get def
  /htrimhi htrimopt 1 get def

  % Wrapped in a 1-element array, the same trick hkgetdef uses for its
  % default: a name bound directly to a procedure auto-executes on
  % every bare reference, which is exactly what /Density holds when
  % the caller passes one. `hdensityopt 0 get` retrieves it as a plain
  % value instead of re-running the caller's callback by accident.
  hopts /Density known { hopts /Density get } { null } ifelse
  1 array astore /hdensityopt exch def
  hdensityopt 0 get null ne
    { hdensityopt 0 get xcheck not { hatch-density-must-be-callable } if }
  if
  hopts /DensityThreshold 0 hkgetdef /hdthresh exch def
  /hnbuckets 6 def

  hopts /MaxLines 20000 hkgetdef /hmaxlines exch def
  hopts /MaxSamples 2000000 hkgetdef /hmaxsamples exch def

  % ---- deterministic pre-flight budget check (no drawing, no RNG) ----
  /htotallines 0 def
  /htotalsamples 0 def
  hangles {
    /hang exch def
    hang 90 add cos /hnx exch def
    hang 90 add sin /hny exch def
    hang cos abs hbw mul hang sin abs hbh mul add /hmaxseg exch def
    hkmmreset
    hbx0 hnx mul hby0 hny mul add hkmmfeed
    hbx1 hnx mul hby0 hny mul add hkmmfeed
    hbx0 hnx mul hby1 hny mul add hkmmfeed
    hbx1 hnx mul hby1 hny mul add hkmmfeed
    hkmx hkmn sub hspacing div cvi 1 add /hnlines exch def
    /htotallines htotallines hnlines add def
    hmaxseg hstep div cvi 1 add /hsamplesperline exch def
    /htotalsamples htotalsamples hnlines hsamplesperline mul add def
  } forall
  htotallines hmaxlines gt { hatch-line-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if
  hdensityopt 0 get null ne htotalsamples hmaxsamples gt and
    { hatch-sample-count-exceeds-safety-limit }
  if

  hopts /Seed known /hseeded exch def
  hseeded { /hsaved rrand def hopts /Seed get cvi srand } if

  % gsave/grestore, not just a bare setlinewidth: every drawing branch
  % below sets its own line width (from /Width or a density bucket),
  % which would otherwise silently overwrite the caller's own
  % linewidth with no way back -- gsave/grestore also protects the
  % current path, which is what lets a second, layered `hatch` call
  % over the same clip still default /BBox from `pathbbox` (see the
  % /BBox docs above): without it, the first call's own strokes end
  % with `stroke`'s ordinary implicit newpath, leaving nothing for a
  % second call's default to read.
  gsave
  hangles {
    /hang exch def
    hang cos /hdx exch def
    hang sin /hdy exch def
    hang 90 add cos /hnx exch def
    hang 90 add sin /hny exch def
    /hcproj hcx hnx mul hcy hny mul add def
    hkmmreset
    hbx0 hnx mul hby0 hny mul add hcproj sub hkmmfeed
    hbx1 hnx mul hby0 hny mul add hcproj sub hkmmfeed
    hbx0 hnx mul hby1 hny mul add hcproj sub hkmmfeed
    hbx1 hnx mul hby1 hny mul add hcproj sub hkmmfeed
    /hkmin hkmn def
    /hkmax hkmx def

    hkmin hspacing hkmax {
      /hk exch def
      hdropout 0 gt { hkfrnd hdropout lt } { false } ifelse
        {}
        {
          hwobble 0 gt { hkfrnd 2 mul 1 sub hwobble mul } { 0 } ifelse
          hk add /hkk exch def
          hcx hnx hkk mul add /hpx exch def
          hcy hny hkk mul add /hpy exch def
          hpx hpy hdx hdy hdiag neg hdiag hkclipseg
          /hhit exch def
          /hthi exch def
          /htlo exch def
          hhit {
            htrimhi 0 gt
              {
                hkfrnd htrimhi htrimlo sub mul htrimlo add /htfrac exch def
                hkfrnd /htsplit exch def
              }
              {
                /htfrac 0 def
                /htsplit 0.5 def
              }
            ifelse
            hthi htlo sub /hspan exch def
            htlo hspan htfrac mul htsplit mul add /htlo2 exch def
            hthi hspan htfrac mul 1 htsplit sub mul sub /hthi2 exch def
            htlo2 hthi2 le {
              hdensityopt 0 get null eq
                {
                  hwidthranged
                    { hkfrnd hwidthhi hwidthlo sub mul hwidthlo add }
                    { hwidthlo }
                  ifelse
                  setlinewidth
                  newpath
                  hcx hnx hkk mul add hdx htlo2 mul add
                  hcy hny hkk mul add hdy htlo2 mul add
                  moveto
                  hcx hnx hkk mul add hdx hthi2 mul add
                  hcy hny hkk mul add hdy hthi2 mul add
                  lineto
                  stroke
                }
                {
                  /hrunb 0 def
                  /hrunt0 htlo2 def
                  htlo2 hstep hthi2 {
                    /ht exch def
                    hcx hnx hkk mul add hdx ht mul add /hsx exch def
                    hcy hny hkk mul add hdy ht mul add /hsy exch def
                    hsx hsy hdensityopt 0 get exec hkclamp01 /hdval exch def
                    hdval hdthresh le
                      { /hb 0 def }
                      { hdval hnbuckets 1 sub mul cvi 1 add /hb exch def }
                    ifelse
                    hb hrunb ne {
                      hrunb 0 gt {
                        hwidthlo hwidthhi hrunb 1 sub hnbuckets 1 sub div hklerp
                        setlinewidth
                        newpath
                        hcx hnx hkk mul add hdx hrunt0 mul add
                        hcy hny hkk mul add hdy hrunt0 mul add
                        moveto
                        hcx hnx hkk mul add hdx ht mul add
                        hcy hny hkk mul add hdy ht mul add
                        lineto
                        stroke
                      } if
                      /hrunt0 ht def
                      /hrunb hb def
                    } if
                  } for
                  hrunb 0 gt {
                    hwidthlo hwidthhi hrunb 1 sub hnbuckets 1 sub div hklerp
                    setlinewidth
                    newpath
                    hcx hnx hkk mul add hdx hrunt0 mul add
                    hcy hny hkk mul add hdy hrunt0 mul add
                    moveto
                    hcx hnx hkk mul add hdx hthi2 mul add
                    hcy hny hkk mul add hdy hthi2 mul add
                    lineto
                    stroke
                  } if
                }
              ifelse
            } if
          } if
        }
      ifelse
    } for
  } forall
  grestore

  hseeded { hsaved srand } if
} def
%%EOF
